1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,160
In the last film, our subsea transition
piece reached its highest stress at the

2
00:00:05,210 --> 00:00:08,721
fillet root. Now increase
the load by seventy percent.

3
00:00:08,771 --> 00:00:13,931
The linear elastic answer at the fillet
is 558 megapascals, far above the yield

4
00:00:13,981 --> 00:00:14,986
strength of 355.

5
00:00:15,267 --> 00:00:19,481
But that number is fiction. Steel
cannot carry stress far beyond yield;

6
00:00:19,531 --> 00:00:24,106
it deforms plastically, and the extra
load flows into the material around it.

7
00:00:24,156 --> 00:00:28,971
The elastic-plastic analysis shows a
plastic zone a few millimetres across, and a

8
00:00:29,021 --> 00:00:31,133
peak plastic strain of 0.18 percent.

9
00:00:31,400 --> 00:00:35,943
So the steel has yielded. Has anything
failed? Answering that takes a different

10
00:00:35,993 --> 00:00:40,711
analysis, and different criteria. Here are
five finite element cases that show how

11
00:00:40,761 --> 00:00:41,699
engineers decide.

12
00:00:43,767 --> 00:00:48,462
Start with the material. Structural steel
yields at a strain of about 0.17 percent,

13
00:00:48,512 --> 00:00:53,264
but it can stretch fifteen percent or more
before it necks, and further still before

14
00:00:53,314 --> 00:00:58,009
it fractures. Yield is the start of the
plastic range, not the end of the material.

15
00:00:58,300 --> 00:01:02,728
And plasticity redistributes stress. In
a beam, the outer fibres yield first.

16
00:01:02,778 --> 00:01:07,440
They stop taking extra stress, so further
moment is carried by the fibres further

17
00:01:07,490 --> 00:01:12,151
in. The stress profile flattens from a
triangle towards a rectangle, and the beam

18
00:01:12,201 --> 00:01:13,314
keeps carrying load.

19
00:01:13,600 --> 00:01:17,438
Once yield is allowed, the failure
modes that matter are different.

20
00:01:17,488 --> 00:01:20,745
Plastic collapse: unbounded
deformation at constant load.

21
00:01:20,795 --> 00:01:24,807
Local failure: ductile fracture where
strain and triaxiality are high.

22
00:01:24,857 --> 00:01:27,767
Ratcheting: strain that
grows every cycle. Fatigue.

23
00:01:27,817 --> 00:01:30,436
And buckling. Yield
itself is not on the list.

24
00:01:30,733 --> 00:01:35,180
Each needs its own analysis and
its own acceptance criterion.

25
00:01:35,230 --> 00:01:41,224
Design-by-analysis rules define them: ASME
Section Eight Division Two and EN 13445

26
00:01:41,274 --> 00:01:45,941
for pressure equipment, and
DNV-RP-C208 for offshore structures.

27
00:01:47,633 --> 00:01:50,795
The tool is elastic-plastic
finite element analysis.

28
00:01:50,845 --> 00:01:54,872
At every Gauss point, the solver
predicts an elastic trial stress.

29
00:01:54,922 --> 00:01:59,813
If it lies outside the von Mises yield
surface, it is returned radially onto the

30
00:01:59,863 --> 00:02:02,963
surface, and plastic strain
grows along the normal.

31
00:02:03,233 --> 00:02:08,014
The load is applied in increments, each
solved by Newton-Raphson iteration with the

32
00:02:08,064 --> 00:02:12,903
consistent tangent. When the structure can
no longer carry more load, the iterations

33
00:02:12,953 --> 00:02:16,453
stop converging: the numerical
signature of plastic collapse.

34
00:02:16,733 --> 00:02:21,431
Two material models are used: elastic,
perfectly plastic for limit-load analysis,

35
00:02:21,481 --> 00:02:26,003
and the true stress-strain curve, with
its yield plateau and strain hardening.

36
00:02:26,300 --> 00:02:32,232
The solver used here was written for this
film and checked against Hill's exact

37
00:02:32,282 --> 00:02:36,624
solution for a thick cylinder
under pressure. First yield:

38
00:02:36,674 --> 00:02:40,941
224 megapascals, against 224.
Collapse: 413, against 414.

39
00:02:41,233 --> 00:02:45,172
These methods move the safety
margin from the stress to the load.

40
00:02:45,222 --> 00:02:50,205
Instead of asking whether a stress is below
an allowable, they multiply the design

41
00:02:50,255 --> 00:02:53,949
loads by a factor and ask whether
the structure still stands:

42
00:02:53,999 --> 00:02:59,043
1.5 for a limit-load analysis, 2.4 for a
full elastic-plastic analysis, and 1.7 for

43
00:02:59,093 --> 00:02:59,841
local strain.

44
00:03:03,633 --> 00:03:09,333
Case one: a 20-millimetre S355 plate, 200
millimetres wide, with a 60-millimetre

45
00:03:09,383 --> 00:03:15,371
hole, in tension. The stress concentration
factor on the net section is 2.36, so the

46
00:03:15,421 --> 00:03:18,894
edge of the hole yields
at about 420 kilonewtons.

47
00:03:19,166 --> 00:03:24,131
Keep loading. Plasticity spreads from the
hole in inclined bands, and the load keeps

48
00:03:24,181 --> 00:03:29,026
rising. Only when the bands reach the edges
of the plate does the curve level off:

49
00:03:29,076 --> 00:03:33,325
the limit load, 996 kilonewtons,
matching yield strength times net area.

50
00:03:33,599 --> 00:03:37,298
First yield occurred at 42
percent of the collapse load.

51
00:03:37,348 --> 00:03:42,587
The criterion is a limit-load analysis.
ASME uses an elastic, perfectly plastic

52
00:03:42,637 --> 00:03:47,608
model with a yield stress of 1.5 times
the allowable stress, and requires a

53
00:03:47,658 --> 00:03:50,888
converged solution at 1.5
times the design loads.

54
00:03:51,166 --> 00:03:56,125
Here, that permits a design load of about
550 kilonewtons, where the elastic peak at

55
00:03:56,175 --> 00:04:00,716
the hole is 460 megapascals: well above
yield, and acceptable against plastic

56
00:04:00,766 --> 00:04:05,725
collapse. It is the same answer as the
classical rule that the average stress on the

57
00:04:05,775 --> 00:04:10,317
net section must not exceed the allowable.
The peak at the hole never enters.

58
00:04:14,197 --> 00:04:19,745
Case two: the constant-moment region of a
steel beam, 100 by 50 millimetres, bent by

59
00:04:19,795 --> 00:04:24,810
a controlled end rotation. The outer
fibres yield at 29.6 kilonewton metres.

60
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:30,141
Plasticity then spreads inward from both
faces, around a shrinking elastic core.

61
00:04:30,431 --> 00:04:34,731
The moment approaches the fully
plastic moment, 44.4 kilonewton metres:

62
00:04:34,781 --> 00:04:38,774
one and a half times first yield,
the shape factor of a rectangle.

63
00:04:38,824 --> 00:04:43,860
The finite element curve follows the
closed-form solution to within a fraction of a

64
00:04:43,910 --> 00:04:44,350
percent.

65
00:04:44,631 --> 00:04:49,419
With the real stress-strain curve, hardening
means the curve never quite flattens.

66
00:04:49,469 --> 00:04:52,546
So where is collapse? The
twice-elastic-slope method:

67
00:04:52,596 --> 00:04:56,086
draw a line from the origin
with half the elastic stiffness.

68
00:04:56,136 --> 00:05:00,984
It cuts the curve at 43.0 kilonewton metres,
the collapse moment, and the allowable

69
00:05:01,034 --> 00:05:02,282
is two-thirds of that.

70
00:05:02,564 --> 00:05:07,657
That two-thirds is no accident. For a
rectangle, two-thirds of the plastic moment is

71
00:05:07,707 --> 00:05:12,738
first yield. It is also why elastic stress
rules allow membrane plus bending stress

72
00:05:12,788 --> 00:05:16,595
up to 1.5 times the allowable:
the shape factor of a rectangle.

73
00:05:16,645 --> 00:05:19,962
Redistribution is already
built into the elastic rules.

74
00:05:23,831 --> 00:05:29,393
Case three: a high-pressure subsea body,
modelled as a thick cylinder with a

75
00:05:29,443 --> 00:05:35,227
100-millimetre bore and a 200-millimetre
outside diameter, in a 75 ksi forging,

76
00:05:35,277 --> 00:05:39,141
rated for 20,000 psi. At
that pressure it is elastic:

77
00:05:39,191 --> 00:05:41,578
the bore reaches 319 megapascals.

78
00:05:41,864 --> 00:05:45,394
The elastic-plastic design
check asks a harder question:

79
00:05:45,444 --> 00:05:48,590
does it still carry 2.4
times the design pressure?

80
00:05:48,640 --> 00:05:53,129
At 48,000 psi, the elastic answer at
the bore would be 766 megapascals.

81
00:05:53,179 --> 00:05:58,243
The elastic-plastic analysis shows the
inner 31 percent of the wall yielded, the

82
00:05:58,293 --> 00:06:03,230
plastic front exactly where Hill's
solution puts it, and a converged solution.

83
00:06:03,280 --> 00:06:03,869
It passes.

84
00:06:04,164 --> 00:06:08,300
Collapse only comes at 413
megapascals, three times the rating.

85
00:06:08,350 --> 00:06:13,881
And when the pressure is released, the
elastic outer wall squeezes the yielded bore,

86
00:06:13,931 --> 00:06:18,996
leaving a residual hoop stress of minus
284 megapascals, the principle behind

87
00:06:19,046 --> 00:06:19,860
autofrettage.

88
00:06:20,131 --> 00:06:25,304
Because the overpressure stayed below twice
the first-yield pressure, unloading is

89
00:06:25,354 --> 00:06:28,616
elastic, and later pressure
cycles stay elastic too.

90
00:06:28,666 --> 00:06:33,902
API 17TR8, for high-pressure subsea equipment,
builds on these same elastic-plastic

91
00:06:33,952 --> 00:06:34,412
methods.

92
00:06:38,297 --> 00:06:44,380
Case four returns to our transition piece,
under an axial stress of 150 megapascals

93
00:06:44,430 --> 00:06:50,440
in the pipe wall. The fillet's stress
concentration factor is 2.19, so the elastic

94
00:06:50,490 --> 00:06:56,351
peak is 329 megapascals, just below yield.
Yield begins at 1.08 times the design

95
00:06:56,401 --> 00:06:56,720
load.

96
00:06:56,997 --> 00:07:02,087
Protection against local failure is checked
by an elastic-plastic analysis at 1.7

97
00:07:02,137 --> 00:07:05,767
times the design loads, with
the true stress-strain curve.

98
00:07:05,817 --> 00:07:11,097
The plastic zone stays at the fillet root,
and the peak equivalent plastic strain is

99
00:07:11,147 --> 00:07:11,921
0.18 percent.

100
00:07:12,197 --> 00:07:17,175
The limit is a strain, adjusted for
triaxiality, because ductility falls as

101
00:07:17,225 --> 00:07:22,739
hydrostatic tension rises. For S355, the
uniaxial strain limit is about 40 percent.

102
00:07:22,789 --> 00:07:26,760
At the fillet's triaxiality of
0.56, it falls to 26 percent.

103
00:07:26,810 --> 00:07:30,849
The demand, plus any strain from
forming, must stay below it.

104
00:07:30,899 --> 00:07:34,804
Here it is 0.18 percent: under
one percent of the capacity.

105
00:07:35,097 --> 00:07:38,992
Neuber's rule, the classic hand
estimate, gives 0.24 percent:

106
00:07:39,042 --> 00:07:44,424
conservative, as expected. At a smooth notch
in ductile steel, local yield is rarely

107
00:07:44,474 --> 00:07:47,140
the problem. Fatigue at that notch can be.

108
00:07:51,031 --> 00:07:55,527
Case five adds time: a thin cylinder under
constant pressure, with a temperature

109
00:07:55,577 --> 00:07:58,425
difference through the wall
that cycles on and off.

110
00:07:58,475 --> 00:08:02,574
This is the classic Bree problem. The
first heat-up yields both surfaces.

111
00:08:02,624 --> 00:08:04,677
What happens next decides everything.

112
00:08:04,964 --> 00:08:09,448
Bree mapped the outcomes on one diagram:
the pressure stress on one axis, the

113
00:08:09,498 --> 00:08:12,450
thermal stress on the other,
both divided by yield.

114
00:08:12,500 --> 00:08:16,042
Below the line where they sum
to one, every cycle is elastic.

115
00:08:16,092 --> 00:08:18,986
Our three analyses sit in
three different regions.

116
00:08:19,264 --> 00:08:24,456
At moderate pressure and thermal load, the
first cycle leaves residual stresses that

117
00:08:24,506 --> 00:08:29,510
keep every later cycle elastic. The plastic
strain stops at 0.14 percent, and the

118
00:08:29,560 --> 00:08:32,693
diameter stays fixed. The
cylinder has shaken down.

119
00:08:32,964 --> 00:08:37,773
With a lower pressure but a larger thermal
range, the surfaces yield in tension and

120
00:08:37,823 --> 00:08:41,109
compression every cycle, but
the diameter stops changing.

121
00:08:41,159 --> 00:08:45,968
That is alternating plasticity: no ratcheting,
but a plastic strain range that must

122
00:08:46,018 --> 00:08:47,958
be assessed for low-cycle fatigue.

123
00:08:48,231 --> 00:08:52,703
Raise the pressure, and the strain steps
forward every cycle, without limit.

124
00:08:52,753 --> 00:08:55,500
That is ratcheting, and
it does end in failure.

125
00:08:55,550 --> 00:08:58,535
The criterion is a cyclic
elastic-plastic analysis:

126
00:08:58,585 --> 00:09:03,236
after a few cycles, there must be shakedown,
an elastic core, or no progressive

127
00:09:03,286 --> 00:09:04,486
change in dimensions.

128
00:09:06,164 --> 00:09:09,674
So here are the criteria.
Against plastic collapse:

129
00:09:09,724 --> 00:09:15,049
a converged limit-load analysis at 1.5
times the loads, or an elastic-plastic

130
00:09:15,099 --> 00:09:20,493
analysis at 2.4 times, or a collapse
load from the twice-elastic-slope method.

131
00:09:20,543 --> 00:09:25,100
EN 13445 adds a five percent limit
on principal structural strain.

132
00:09:25,397 --> 00:09:30,233
Against local failure: plastic strain below
the triaxial strain limit, at 1.7 times

133
00:09:30,283 --> 00:09:34,825
the loads. Against ratcheting: shakedown,
or an elastic core, under the cyclic

134
00:09:34,875 --> 00:09:39,534
loads. Fatigue, buckling and fracture
keep their own checks, and serviceability,

135
00:09:39,584 --> 00:09:43,595
such as a flange that must stay
tight, can limit deformation further.

136
00:09:43,864 --> 00:09:48,399
Notice what is not on the list: the peak
von Mises stress from a linear elastic

137
00:09:48,449 --> 00:09:53,100
analysis. Yielding is allowed. Collapse,
rupture, ratcheting and fatigue are not.

138
00:09:53,397 --> 00:09:56,733
When the elastic contour turns
red, the analysis is not over.

139
00:09:56,783 --> 00:10:01,395
Ask which failure mode you are protecting
against, and run the analysis that can see

140
00:10:01,445 --> 00:10:01,561
it.

